{"description":"Four version axes disagreed with their own sources. (a) eka-cli README advertised CLI v1.10.0, EKA Standard 1.1, eka-core v1.7.0 while go.mod required eka-core v1.10.5 and the embedded standard declared 1.3. (b) A second README passage advertised EKA Standard 1.1 in the version-command description. (c) eka-mcp skills/manifest.yaml declared requiresEka \"\u003e= 1.16.0\" while the shipping server is 1.15.1 — the pack required a CLI newer than the release containing it. Note that eka version --json already derives all six axes from single sources (standardembed, exchange.SerializationVersion, exchange.ExchangeFormatVersion, machine.Schema, metadata.SchemaVersion, ldflags version); the drift was entirely in hand-written prose and an independent literal, not in the code path.","impact":"A reader told to trust the documentation is misinformed about which standard version the tool implements, and the pack's own instruction (check the local CLI against requiresEka before trusting the pack) made a user reject the release that shipped it. Fix: correct the README claims, derive the pack requirement from the shipping release, and lock both with tests — README claims are parsed and compared against the embedded declaration, go.mod's required eka-core version, and exchange.SpecificationVersion; the pack requirement is compared against pack.Version in both directions so it can neither exceed nor trail the release."}